ntent":"The proto_convert transform can be used to convert protobuf messages to JSON for easier processing"},"539":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sdk-39","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sdk#usage-notes","content":"Field mappings allow you to rename fields during format conversion"},"540":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sdk-40","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sdk#usage-notes","content":"Protocol Buffers provide efficient binary serialization compared to JSON"},"541":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","type":"page","content":"About","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Introduction"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/introduction/about"},"542":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-0","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/introduction/about#features","content":"Features"},"543":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-1","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/introduction/about#supported-languages-sdk","content":"Supported languages SDK"},"544":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-2","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/introduction/about#cli","content":"CLI"},"545":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-3","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/introduction/about#web-ui","content":"Web UI"},"546":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-4","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/introduction/about#connectors","content":"Connectors"},"547":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-5","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/introduction/about#model-context-protocol","content":"Model Context Protocol"},"548":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-6","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/introduction/about#docker","content":"Docker"},"549":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-7","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/introduction/about#helm-charts","content":"Helm Charts"},"550":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-8","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/introduction/about#versioning","content":"Versioning"},"551":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-9","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about","content":"Iggy is a persistent message streaming platform written in Rust, supporting QUIC, TCP, WebSocket (custom binary specification) and HTTP (regular REST API) transport protocols, capable of processing millions of messages per second at ultra-low latency."},"552":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-10","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about","content":"Iggy provides exceptionally high throughput and performance while utilizing minimal computing resources."},"553":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-11","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about","content":"This is not yet another extension running on top of existing infrastructure, such as Kafka or SQL database."},"554":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-12","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about","content":"Iggy is a persistent message streaming log built from the ground up using low-level I/O with thread-per-core shared nothing architecture, io_uring and compio for maximum speed and efficiency."},"555":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-13","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about","content":"The name is an abbreviation for the Italian Greyhound - small yet extremely fast dogs, the best in their class. See the lovely Fabio & Cookie ❤️"},"556":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-14","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#features","content":"Highly performant, persistent append-only log for message streaming"},"557":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-15","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#features","content":"Very high throughput for both writes and reads"},"558":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-16","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#features","content":"Low latency and predictable resource usage thanks to the Rust compiled language (no GC) and io_uring"},"559":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-17","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#features","content":"Thread per core shared nothing design together with io_uring guarantee the best possible performance on modern Linux systems"},"560":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-18","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#features","content":"NUMA-aware sharding with CPU affinity and memory binding per NUMA node for optimal hardware utilization"},"561":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-19","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#features","content":"User authentication and authorization with granular permissions, Personal Access Tokens (PAT) and Argon2id password hashing"},"562":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-20","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#features","content":"Support for multiple streams, topics and partitions"},"563":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-21","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#features","content":"Support for multiple transport protocols (QUIC, TCP, WebSocket, HTTP)"},"564":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-22","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#features","content":"Fully operational RESTful API which can be optionally enabled"},"565":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-23","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#features","content":"Available client SDK in multiple languages"},"566":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-24","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#features","content":"Works directly with binary data, avoiding enforced schema and serialization/deserialization overhead"},"567":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-25","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#features","content":"Custom zero-copy (de)serialization, which greatly improves the performance and reduces memory usage"},"568":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-26","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#features","content":"Custom memory pool with 32 buckets (buffer sizes from 256 B to 512 MiB) for pre-allocated, zero-copy message passing"},"569":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-27","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#features","content":"Configurable server features (e.g. caching, segment size, data flush interval, transport protocols etc.)"},"570":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-28","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#features","content":"Server-side storage of consumer offsets"},"571":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-29","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#features","content":"Multiple ways of polling the messages:"},"572":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-30","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#features","content":"By offset (using the indexes)"},"573":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-31","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#features","content":"By timestamp (using the time indexes)"},"574":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-32","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#features","content":"First/Last N messages"},"575":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-33","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#features","content":"Next N messages for the specific consumer"},"576":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-34","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#features","content":"Possibility of auto committing the offset (e.g. to achieve at-most-once delivery)"},"577":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-35","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#features","content":"Consumer groups providing the message ordering and horizontal scaling across the connected clients, with cooperative partition rebalancing"},"578":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-36","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#features","content":"Message expiry with auto deletion based on the configurable retention policy"},"579":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-37","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#features","content":"Additional features such as server side message deduplication"},"580":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-38","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#features","content":"Multi-tenant support via abstraction of streams which group topics"},"581":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-39","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#features","content":"TLS support for all transport protocols (TCP, QUIC, WebSocket, HTTPS)"},"582":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-40","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"t