rategies","content":"Send to a specific partition"},"924":{"id":"/docs/sdk/introduction-97","page_id":"/docs/sdk/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/introduction#partitioning-strategies","content":"balanced()"},"925":{"id":"/docs/sdk/introduction-98","page_id":"/docs/sdk/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/introduction#partitioning-strategies","content":"Round-robin across partitions"},"926":{"id":"/docs/sdk/introduction-99","page_id":"/docs/sdk/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/introduction#partitioning-strategies","content":"messages_key(key)"},"927":{"id":"/docs/sdk/introduction-100","page_id":"/docs/sdk/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/introduction#partitioning-strategies","content":"Hash-based routing by key"},"928":{"id":"/docs/server/benchmarking","page_id":"/docs/server/benchmarking","type":"page","content":"Benchmarking","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Server"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/benchmarking"},"929":{"id":"/docs/server/benchmarking-0","page_id":"/docs/server/benchmarking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/benchmarking","content":"Benchmarks should be the first-class citizens. We believe that performance is crucial for any system, and we strive to provide the best possible performance for our users. Please check, why we believe that the transparent benchmarking is so important."},"930":{"id":"/docs/server/benchmarking-1","page_id":"/docs/server/benchmarking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/benchmarking","content":"We've also built the benchmarking platform where anyone can upload the benchmarks and compare the results with others. This is the another open-source project available here."},"931":{"id":"/docs/server/benchmarking-2","page_id":"/docs/server/benchmarking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/benchmarking","content":"Iggy comes with a built-in benchmarking tool iggy-bench that allows you to measure the performance of the server. It is written in Rust and uses the tokio runtime for asynchronous I/O operations mimicking the example client applications.  The tool is designed to be as close to real-world scenarios as possible, so you can use it to estimate the performance of the server in your environment. It is part of the core repository and can be found in the bench directory."},"932":{"id":"/docs/server/benchmarking-3","page_id":"/docs/server/benchmarking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/benchmarking","content":"To benchmark the project, first build the project in release mode:"},"933":{"id":"/docs/server/benchmarking-4","page_id":"/docs/server/benchmarking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/benchmarking","content":"Then, run the benchmarking app with the desired options:"},"934":{"id":"/docs/server/benchmarking-5","page_id":"/docs/server/benchmarking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/benchmarking","content":"Sending (writing) benchmark"},"935":{"id":"/docs/server/benchmarking-6","page_id":"/docs/server/benchmarking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/benchmarking","content":"Polling (reading) benchmark"},"936":{"id":"/docs/server/benchmarking-7","page_id":"/docs/server/benchmarking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/benchmarking","content":"Parallel sending and polling benchmark"},"937":{"id":"/docs/server/benchmarking-8","page_id":"/docs/server/benchmarking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/benchmarking","content":"Balanced sending to multiple partitions benchmark"},"938":{"id":"/docs/server/benchmarking-9","page_id":"/docs/server/benchmarking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/benchmarking","content":"Consumer group polling benchmark:"},"939":{"id":"/docs/server/benchmarking-10","page_id":"/docs/server/benchmarking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/benchmarking","content":"Parallel balanced sending and polling from consumer group benchmark:"},"940":{"id":"/docs/server/benchmarking-11","page_id":"/docs/server/benchmarking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/benchmarking","content":"End to end producing and consuming benchmark (single task produces and consumes messages in sequence):"},"941":{"id":"/docs/server/benchmarking-12","page_id":"/docs/server/benchmarking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/benchmarking","content":"These benchmarks would start the server with the default configuration, create a stream, topic and partition, and then send or poll the messages. The default configuration is optimized for the best performance, so you might want to tweak it for your needs. If you need more options, please refer to iggy-bench subcommands help and examples."},"942":{"id":"/docs/server/benchmarking-13","page_id":"/docs/server/benchmarking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/benchmarking","content":"For example, to run the benchmark for the already started server, provide the additional argument --server-address 0.0.0.0:8090."},"943":{"id":"/docs/server/benchmarking-14","page_id":"/docs/server/benchmarking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/benchmarking","content":"Depending on the hardware, transport protocol (quic, tcp or http) and payload size (messages-per-batch * message-size) you might expect over 5000 MB/s (e.g. 5M of 1 KB msg/sec) throughput for writes and reads."},"944":{"id":"/docs/server/benchmarking-15","page_id":"/docs/server/benchmarking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/benchmarking","content":"Iggy is already capable of processing millions of messages per second at the microseconds range for p99+ latency, and with the upcoming optimizations related to the io_uring support along with the shared-nothing design, it will only get better."},"945":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","type":"page","content":"Configuration","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Server"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/configuration"},"946":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-0","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/configuration#environment-variables","content":"Environment variables"},"947":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-1","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/configuration#command-line-arguments","content":"Command-line arguments"},"948":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-2","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/configuration#durability","content":"Durability"},"949":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-3","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/configuration#sharding","content":"Sharding"},"950":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-4","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/configuration#memory-pool","content":"Memory pool"},"951":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-5","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/configuration#clustering","content":"Clustering"},"952":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-6","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/configuration#http","content":"HTTP"},"953":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-7","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/configuration#tcp","content":"TCP"},"954":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-8","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/configuration#quic","content":"QUIC"},"955":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-9","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/configuration#websocket","content":"WebSocket"},"956":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-10","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/configuration#telemetry","content":"Telemetry"},"957":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-11","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration","content":"The server reads its configuration from core/server/config.toml by default. You can specify a different path by setting the IGGY_CONFIG_PATH environment variable (e.g., export IGGY_CONFIG_PATH=/path/to/config.toml). You can also use a .env file to override specific settings using the IGGY_ prefix (e.g., IGGY_HTTP_ENABLED=true)."},"958":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-12","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration","content":"If no configuration file is found, the server uses its built-in defaults."},"959":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-13","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration","content":"Let's take a look at the available configuration options. The following example shows all settings with their default values:"},"960":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-14","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration","content":"Note: For the most up-to-date configuration reference, please refer to the config.toml file in the project's repository."},"961":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-15","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#environment-variables","content":"Every configuration option can be overridden using environment variables with the IGGY_ prefix. The naming convention follows the TOML structure with underscores:"},"962":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-16","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#command-line-arguments","content":"The server binary accepts the following arguments:"},"963":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-17","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#command-line-arguments","content":"Argument"},"964":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-18","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#command-line-arguments","content":"Description"},"965":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-19","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#command-line-arguments","content":"--config-provider file"},"966":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-20","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#command-line-arguments","content":"Configuration source (default: file)"},"967":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-21","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#command-line-arguments","content":"--fresh"},"968":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-22","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#command-line-arguments","content":"Wipe data directory before start"},"969":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-23","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#command-line-arguments","content":"--with-default-root-credentials"},"970":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-24","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#command-line-arguments","content":"Set root to iggy/iggy (dev only)"},"971":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-25","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#command-line-arguments","content":"--follower"},"972":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-26","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#command-line-arguments","content":"Advertise as follower node (clustering)"},"973":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-27","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#durability","content":"The single node Iggy server by default provides low durability guarantees, meaning that in case of a crash or power loss, some of messages might be lost.\nTo fix that you can enable enforce_fsync option in system.partition section together with setting messages_required_to_save and size_of_messages_required_to_save to lowest allowed values (minimum of 1 for count, and 512 B for size). In case if your message size is not big enough, you might want to pad your message to 512 B boundary, or alternatively send messages in batches."},"974":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-28","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#durability","content":"For even stronger durability, enable the message_saver with enforce_fsync = true which provides a background fsync at a configurable interval (default 30s)."},"975":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-29","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#sharding","content":"The [system.sharding] section controls how the server maps shards to CPU cores. This is key to the thread-per-core architecture:"},"976":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-30","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#sharding","content":"\"all\" - one shard per available CPU core"},"977":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-31","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#sharding","content":"A numeric value (e.g. 4) - exactly N shards"},"978":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-32","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#sharding","content":"A range (e.g. \"5..8\") - shards on cores 5, 6, 7"},"979":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-33","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#sharding","content":"\"numa:auto\" - automatic NUMA-aware allocation"},"980":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-34","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#sharding","content":"\"numa:nodes=0,1;cores=4;no_ht=true\" - fine-grained NUMA control"},"981":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-35","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#sharding","content":"See Architecture for more details on the thread-per-core design."},"982":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-36","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#memory-pool","content":"The [system.memory_pool] section configures the pre-allocated buffer pool used for zero-copy message passing:"},"983":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-37","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#memory-pool","content":"size - total pool size (minimum 512 MiB, must be page-aligned to 4096 bytes)"},"984":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-38","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#memory-pool","content":"bucket_capacity - buffers per bucket (must be power of 2, minimum 128)"},"985":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-39","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#memory-pool","content":"The pool has 32 buckets with buffer sizes from 256 B to 512 MiB"},"986":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-40","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#clustering","content":"The [cluster] section configures multi-node replication using Viewstamped Replication (VSR). See Clustering for details. When clustering is enabled, all nodes must share the same cluster name."},"987":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-41","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#http","content":"An optional transport layer, which can be used to connect to the server with any tool or application that supports HTTP. It is enabled by default, but you can disable it by setting enabled to false. The address option specifies the address and port to listen on. The cors section allows you to configure the CORS policy, which internally uses Tower middleware."},"988":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-42","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#http","content":"HTTP API is built on top of Axum and you can find all the available endpoints in server.http file, which can be used with REST Client extension for VS Code."},"989":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-43","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#http","content":"To consume the HTTP API from Iggy SDK, you need to make use of the available HttpClient component."},"990":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-44","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#http","content":"HTTP API is highly performant, however, it's not as performant as TCP, and, since it's a stateless protocol, there's no such thing as the ongoing connection between client and server, thus some of the features such as consumer groups are not available."},"991":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-45","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#http","content":"The HTTP server also hosts Prometheus metrics at /metrics (configurable) and the optional embedded Web UI at /ui (when compiled with iggy-web feature and web_ui = true)."},"992":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-46","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#tcp","content":"An optional transport layer, which can be used to connect to the server with any tool or application that supports TCP. It is enabled by default, but you can disable it by setting enabled to false. The address option specifies the address and port to listen on."},"993":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-47","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#tcp","content":"TCP provides the highest performance and throughput as it works directly with the binary data without an additional overhead of e.g. HTTP spec along with the JSON serialization."},"994":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-48","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#tcp","content":"When socket_migration is enabled (default), TCP sockets can be migrated between shards to avoid cross-shard forwarding of data plane operations."},"995":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-49","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#tcp","content":"To consume the TCP API from Iggy SDK, you need to make use of the available TcpClient component."},"996":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-50","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#quic","content":"An optional transport layer, which can be used to connect to the server with any tool or application that supports QUIC. It is enabled by default, but you can disable it by setting enabled to false. The address option specifies the address and port to listen on."},"997":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-51","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#quic","content":"Similar to TCP it's a stateful protocol, however, it's not as performant as TCP. QUIC support is built on top of Quinn library, and all the remaining options are essentially the direct mapping of the options available in Quinn. QUIC and TCP use the same custom binary protocol specification. QUIC has built-in mandatory TLS encryption."},"998":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-52","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#quic","content":"To consume the QUIC API from Iggy SDK, you need to make use of the available QuicClient component."},"999":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-53","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#websocket","content":"An optional transport layer, which can be used to connect to the server with any tool or application that supports WebSocket. It is enabled by default, but you can disable it by setting enabled to false. The address option specifies the address and port to listen on."},"1000":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-54","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#websocket","content":"WebSocket uses a custom compio-ws implementation that bridges tungstenite with compio's completion-based I/O model. It runs on all shards and supports optional TLS."},"1001":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-55","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#websocket","content":"To consume the WebSocket API from Iggy SDK, you need to make use of the available WebSocketClient component."},"1002":{"id":"/docs/server/configuration-56","page_id":"/docs/server/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/configuration#telemetry","content":"The [telemetry] section configures OpenTelemetry integration for logs and traces. Supports both gRPC and HTTP transports for sending data to your observability backend (e.g. Jaeger, Grafana Tempo, Loki)."},"1003":{"id":"/docs/server/docker","page_id":"/docs/server/docker","type":"page","content":"Docker & Helm","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Server"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/docker"},"1004":{"id":"/docs/server/docker-0","page_id":"/docs/server/docker","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/docker#docker","content":"Docker"},"1005":{"id":"/docs/server/docker-1","page_id":"/docs/server/docker","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/docker#why-these-capabilities","content":"Why these capabilities?"},"1006":{"id":"/docs/server/docker-2","page_id":"/docs/server/docker","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/docker#available-images","content":"Available images"},"1007":{"id":"/docs/server/docker-3","page_id":"/docs/server/docker","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/docker#building-from-source","content":"Building from source"},"1008":{"id":"/docs/server/docker-4","page_id":"/docs/server/docker","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/docker#running-the-cli-inside-the-container","content":"Running the CLI inside the container"},"1009":{"id":"/docs/server/docker-5","page_id":"/docs/server/docker","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/docker#helm-charts","content":"Helm charts"},"1010":{"id":"/docs/server/docker-6","page_id":"/docs/server/docker","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/docker#quick-start","content":"Quick start"},"1011":{"id":"/docs/server/docker-7","page_id":"/docs/server/docker","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/docker#chart-components","content":"Chart components"},"1012":{"id":"/docs/server/docker-8","page_id":"/docs/server/docker","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/server/docker#key-values","content":"Key values"},"1013":{"id":"/docs/server/docker-9","page_id":"/docs/server/docker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/docker#docker","content":"You can easily run the Iggy server with Docker - the official images can be found here, simply type docker pull apache/iggy."},"1014":{"id":"/docs/server/docker-10","page_id":"/docs/server/docker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/docker#docker","content":"Below is an example of the docker-compose.yml file which additionally overrides the default configuration (described in the section below) with the environment variables. If you prefer using the configuration file, you can mount it as a volume and provide the path to it with the IGGY_CONFIG_PATH environment variable."},"1015":{"id":"/docs/server/docker-11","page_id":"/docs/server/docker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/docker#docker","content":"When running the container, make sure to include the additional capabilities required by io_uring and CPU affinity:"},"1016":{"id":"/docs/server/docker-12","page_id":"/docs/server/docker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/docker#docker","content":"Or when running with docker run:"},"1017":{"id":"/docs/server/docker-13","page_id":"/docs/server/docker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/docker#why-these-capabilities","content":"SYS_NICE - required for setting CPU affinity (sched_setaffinity) in the thread-per-core architecture"},"1018":{"id":"/docs/server/docker-14","page_id":"/docs/server/docker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/docker#why-these-capabilities","content":"seccomp:unconfined - required for io_uring syscalls which are blocked by Docker's default seccomp profile"},"1019":{"id":"/docs/server/docker-15","page_id":"/docs/server/docker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/docker#why-these-capabilities","content":"memlock: -1 - io_uring needs to lock memory pages shared between user space and kernel"},"1020":{"id":"/docs/server/docker-16","page_id":"/docs/server/docker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/docker#available-images","content":"Image"},"1021":{"id":"/docs/server/docker-17","page_id":"/docs/server/docker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/docker#available-images","content":"Description"},"1022":{"id":"/docs/server/docker-18","page_id":"/docs/server/docker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/docker#available-images","content":"apache/iggy"},"1023":{"id":"/docs/server/docker-19","page_id":"/docs/server/docker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/docker#available-images","content":"Server + CLI"},"1024":{"id":"/docs/server/docker-20","page_id":"/docs/server/docker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/server/docker#available-images","content":"apache/iggy-web-ui"},"1025":{"id":"/docs/server/docker-21","page